  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-RAF108</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved capacities of regional and sub regional constituents to promote fundamental principles and rights at work in Africa</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Africa has the highest number of children in child labour globally, with an estimated 87 million children in 2024&#x2014;nearly two thirds of the worldwide total. Achieving fair labour markets and improved human development outcomes for these children requires strong policies and effective legislation.
This regional programme aims to accelerate the elimination of child labour and strengthen the implementation of Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work (FPRW) across Africa. It builds the capacity of national focal points and social partners to apply international labour standards related to child labour, forced labour, non-discrimination, freedom of association, collective bargaining, and occupational safety and health.
The programme also engages parliamentarians within continental bodies and African Union Commission (AUC) organs to enhance understanding and promotion of child labour conventions and recommendations. Its implementation is supported by the Accelerating action for the elimination of child labour in supply chains in Africa (ACCEL Africa) project.
In parallel, a specialized regional initiative targeting children in transit is being implemented by a coalition of regional and international organizations. This programme delivers integrated, cross-border assistance to vulnerable children.
Overall, the programme contributes to the Marrakech Global Framework for Action Against Child Labour and supports Member States in creating safer, more equitable and protective environments for children. It strengthens capacities, fosters partnerships, and promotes effective policy action. During the biennium, expected outcomes include improved regional strategies, policies, programmes, and coordination mechanisms to address child labour. Primary beneficiaries are ILO constituents in Africa, with collaboration from the African Union Commission, the African Committee of Experts on the Rights and Welfare of the Child (ACERWC), and regional economic communities.</narrative>
  </description>
